  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThere's not much to say about the long-term pattern that hasn't \nalready been said. The subtropical ridge will continue to dominate \nfrom the Gulf Coast to the southern Plains through the upcoming \nweek. There will be a surge of monsoonal moisture from the Gulf of \nCalifornia northward through Arizona/New Mexico and northeastward \ninto the central Plains. This pattern will encourage thunderstorm \ndevelopment daily throughout the plume. However, for most of us, \nsubsidence from the subtropical ridge looks likely to keep all rain \nand cooler temperatures away at least through the middle of next \nweek.\n\nThe potential exception to the rule is northwest Oklahoma. Here, \nthere is a little more distance from the center of the subtropical \nhigh. There's also a little less distance to the Rocky Mountain \nterrain features that will initiate daily thunderstorms from Friday \nthrough the weekend. Thus, low-end nocturnal precipitation chances \nremain in the forecast in far northwest OK from Friday night through \nMonday night. For the rest of us, expect hot, dry, and sunny weather \nto continue.\n\nMeister",
